 completing smaller  quicker Street crimes because you'll  quickly want the reward these challenges  Dole out the game soon descends into  hours of completing fourth forgettable  side missions that slightly change the  context of each crime but never really  the actions you perform to stop them it  would genuinely be exhausting to patrol  such a Relentless troubled City each and  every night knowing there's really no  end in sight but that likely wasn't the  intention here and it doesn't make for a  good video game worse once you have  those Knighthood challenges completed  you'll still bump up against these  forgettable side missions frequently  boss storylines are Level gated so you  can't quickly see the end of any  questline and to level up between major  boss missions you'll often be doing more  of these Street crimes meanwhile iconic  characters like the Penguin and Lucius  Fox are made to be nothing more than  immobile Quest givers darling out  missions that are more like basic  challenges to earn a new suit blueprint  or a mess of crafting resources  if you really want to go down this  Rabbit Hole you need to know there's no  coming back from it  becoming Gotham City's watchful  protector should be a meaningful Journey  for the characters and the players  controlling them but it rarely amounts  to feeling like anything more than busy  work there are curiously few big bads in  the game too and they lack the variety  and Ingenuity of past showdowns even in  developer WB Montreal's past bat efforts  aside from the main storyline that  heavily involves the League of Shadows  and the court of owls you'll do battle  with Harley Clayface and Mr Freeze I'd  have less of an issue with this low  number if the actual showdowns were more  diverse or interesting but they each  inevitably devolve into an arena battle  against a damaged spongy villain and  maybe an intermittent horde of goons  like the mission design and writing  combat initially makes a strong  impression by being distinct from  previous Batman games the problem  however is it fails to evolve as time  goes on it swaps out the active counter  heavy model for something more rpg-like  where cooldowns and a momentum meet a  lord over your capabilities and it can  look cool for a while you'll stylishly  take down foes of a few different  classes but rather than Occam's very  defensive minded combat here it's more  about keeping the pressure on and always  hitting something it overrides the  Learned techniques that Batman must have  taught the foursome in the name of being  an RPG that demands 30 enemies who  artificially extend each brawl with  hearty health bars as brawls play out it  also reveals a lack of variety moves  look different for each character to  match their personality but the button  presses you'll perform follow the same  script no matter who you are each  character's unique special moves are the  only thing that really separates the  heroes from each other the court of owls  perhaps the biggest and most beloved new  addition to the Batman Mythos in a long  time fails to live up to the potential  of his comic book Source material in the  comics the core is a creepy ever-present  threat whose existence is Up For Debate  merely Whispering about them could get  you killed or so say the dark nursery  rhymes that element of the villainous  secret society is touched on here but it  all feels like lip service in practice  the court feels like any other faction  of enemies especially when you so often  come across them taking part in Street  crimes thereby totally neglecting the  cabal's most important characteristic  they're working from the Shadows that's  not to say WB Montreal misunderstands  its cast of characters although the  court is narratively fumbled the game's  brightest spot is certainly its foursome  of Heroes Batgirl Red Hood Robin and  Nightwing are handed the keys to the  city without warning and they're not  sure how to fill Batman's Boots the  interactions are very often somber and  serious and help characterize them as  The Grieving unsure group of Orphans  they are Batman meant something  different to each of them and the  writing captures this excellently  leveling up and playing with any of them  unlocks the same skill points for the  others which invites mixing in time with  each of them swapping between characters  doesn't just give you a new skill tree  it gives you a new perspective on this  Larger than Life figure who is now  suddenly an irreversibly absent it lands  as hard as intended and it's compelling  to see them pick up the pieces and grow  together  you could visit them  I could what  he went to yours when  you could visit his grave that growing  together is best done in Co-op where the  game can be played with one other person  this version of Gotham is not its best  rendition but it is massive and benefits  from the basic video game truth that  most everything is better in Co-op  Gotham Knight smartly accounts for Co-Op  play in a number of ways too main story  progress follows the host's lead so  opening the map as either player will  reveal the host's current to-do list  however anything the second player does  carries over into their native save data  including gear they found and even story  missions they've completed you'll always  have the option to skip and get credit  for Missions you've already completed in  Co-op this is a great detail that too  few of these Co-op optional games  Overlook so even though Gotham Knight  isn't a great game in most respects it  does at least reveal a better path  forward for games like it it's too bad  the city isn't more exciting because  with a welcome lack of Co-op tethering  defending Gotham in Co-op could take  place miles apart instead there's little  reason to separate though as peripheral  content is so low that you'll want to  only do what's required of you to unlock  the major next story be and nothing more  there's even a peculiar lack of end game  content normally these loot Centric  games are built on the Long Haul but  here the game has all the markings of a  loot ground with any purpose to perform  it if my gear is good enough to beat the  final boss and it was then what  incentive do I have to keep playing to  improve that gear I suspect the  forthcoming four-player Co-op heroic  assault mode which is launching in late  November may be a better answer to this  question but waiting five weeks to go  back to a game that isn't very good in  the first place isn't in my plans by the  end the project as a whole feels  compromised and instead of feeling like  the triumphant journey of iconic Heroes  the Lasting Impression is one of a  difficult development cycle the sudden  cancellation of the last gen versions  was perhaps the first sign but more  appear as you play this includes a lack  of 60 frames per second support on  consoles frame drops in Solo or  multiplayer and an oddly High number of  hidden loading screens if feels old  already even as it can still look quite  lovely when perched high above the city  as its tireless Guardian under the hood  I suspect the game is chugging along to  make ends meet and it certainly feels  that way to play it Gotham Knight's acts  more as a cautionary tale than the  logical next step for this storied  Universe for a long time no one knew how  to make a compelling Batman game and  then we got four of them in seven years  taking what worked before while seeking  to evolve it is admirable but the  obsession over loot is ill-conceived  cynical and tasteless it feels as though  volume of content is given precedence  over meaningful content and for those  that might enjoy the grind the end  game's absence will be noticeable strong  character work and a well-considered  co-op experience can't save all that  ales Gotham Knights leaving this  experience neither the one fans deserve  nor the one they need right now  [Music]

